GUI (Graphical User Interface) is one of the most important developments in computer history. It changed computers from machines operated by specialists into tools that ordinary people could use.

A simple definition:

> **A GUI is a way for humans to interact with computers through visual elements such as windows, icons, buttons, menus, and pointers instead of typing text commands.**

Before GUI:

```text
User
 |
 | type commands
 v
Command Line Interface (CLI)

$ copy file1.txt file2.txt
$ mkdir project
$ gcc main.c
```

After GUI:

```text
User
 |
 | click, drag, type
 v
GUI

[ File ] [ Edit ] [ View ]

+----------------+
|   document     |
|                |
+----------------+
```

---

# 1. What existed before GUI?

Early computers did not have screens like today's computers.

## 1940s-1950s: Punch cards and batch processing

Users prepared programs using:

* punch cards
* paper tape

Example:

```
Program cards
      |
      v
Computer operator
      |
      v
Computer runs program
      |
      v
Output printed
```

There was almost no interaction.

The programmer did not "talk" to the computer directly.

---

## 1960s: Command line interface

Computers gained terminals:

```
Keyboard
    |
    v
+----------+
| Terminal |
+----------+
    |
    v
Computer
```

Users typed commands:

```bash
ls
cd project
compile program.c
```

This was much better, but still required learning many commands.

A beginner needed to know:

```
mkdir
chmod
grep
awk
sed
```

The computer was powerful, but unfriendly.

---

# 2. The birth of GUI

The idea of GUI came from research at:

Xerox PARC

during the 1970s.

Researchers asked:

> "What if people could interact with computers like they interact with physical objects?"

They created concepts that became fundamental:

* windows
* icons
* mouse
* menus
* desktop metaphor

---

## Xerox Alto (1973)

The Xerox Alto was one of the first computers with a modern GUI.

It introduced:

### Window

Instead of one text screen:

```
command
command
command
```

you could have:

```
+--------------+
| Document     |
|              |
+--------------+

+--------------+
| Calculator   |
|              |
+--------------+
```

---

### Mouse

Before:

```
type coordinates:
move cursor 20,30
```

After:

```
move pointer
click object
```

The mouse became a human-friendly pointing device.

---

### Icons

Instead of:

```
delete file.txt
```

you could:

```
drag file icon
        |
        v
     trash icon
```

---

# 3. GUI becomes popular

## Apple Macintosh (1984)

Macintosh 128K made GUI popular among ordinary consumers.

Apple introduced:

* desktop
* folders
* menus
* mouse
* icons

The famous idea:

> "The computer should adapt to humans, not humans adapt to computers."

---

## Microsoft Windows

Microsoft Windows brought GUI to a much larger market.

Important versions:

* Windows 3.1 (1992)
* Windows 95 (1995)
* Windows XP (2001)

Windows made GUI the standard interface for personal computers.

---

# 4. Why is GUI so popular?

The main reason:

## Humans are visual creatures

A command line requires remembering:

```
cp source destination
mv old new
rm filename
```

GUI uses recognition:

```
See file
↓
Click file
↓
Choose action
```

Psychologically:

### CLI:

"Remember the command"

### GUI:

"Recognize the object"

Humans are much better at recognition than memorization.

---

# 5. GUI reduces the learning barrier

Imagine a photo editor.

CLI:

```
convert image.jpg -resize 800x600 output.jpg
```

GUI:

```
Open image
     |
Click Resize
     |
Choose size
     |
Save
```

A person does not need to understand commands.

---

# 6. Relationship between GUI and computer programming

GUI did not replace programming.

Instead:

> GUI created a new type of programming problem.

Before GUI:

Programs were mostly:

```
Input
 |
Process
 |
Output
```

Example:

```c
scanf("%d", &x);
calculate();
printf("%d", result);
```

This is a **linear program**.

---

GUI programs are event-driven:

```
             User clicks button
                    |
                    v
              Event generated
                    |
                    v
             Program responds
```

Example:

```java
button.addActionListener(e -> {
    calculate();
});
```

The program waits for events.

---

# 7. GUI introduced event-driven programming

This was a huge change.

Traditional program:

```c
main()
{
    read_input();

    calculate();

    print_result();
}
```

The programmer controls the flow.

---

GUI program:

```java
main()
{
    create_window();

    create_button();

    wait_forever();
}


button_clicked()
{
    calculate();
}
```

Now:

* user controls the flow
* computer reacts

This changed software architecture.

---

# 8. GUI created new programming concepts

## Object-oriented programming

This connects to your previous question about OOP.

GUI heavily encouraged OOP.

Why?

Because GUI elements naturally behave like objects.

Example:

A button:

```java
Button loginButton;
```

has:

Properties:

```
color
size
position
text
```

Methods:

```
click()
enable()
disable()
```

A window:

```java
Window mainWindow;
```

has:

```
title
size
children
events
```

This maps naturally to objects.

---

## Components

Modern GUI is built from reusable components:

```
Application

+----------------+
| Window         |
|                |
| +------------+ |
| | Button     | |
| +------------+ |
|                |
| +------------+ |
| | TextBox    | |
| +------------+ |
+----------------+
```

Programming became assembling objects.

